# Code owners for verifiable-git-infrastructure.
#
# GitHub requests a review from the owning team on every pull request that
# touches a matching path. On its own this file only *requests* those reviews:
# it blocks nothing until a ruleset on `main` turns on "require review from
# Code Owners", which is a repository setting rather than a file in the tree.
#
# The teams are the ones CLOWarden maintains for this repository from
# OpenVTC/governance `config.yaml`: openvtc-admins (admin) and
# openvtc-maintainers (maintain). A team named here must have write access to
# the repository, so lf-staff is deliberately absent — it holds no grant on
# this repository, and naming it would make the whole file invalid.
#
# Later entries win, so the catch-all comes first.

*                                   @OpenVTC/openvtc-admins @OpenVTC/openvtc-maintainers

# Release and CI plumbing. These workflows hold the crates.io OIDC permission
# and write access to Releases, and the composite action under .github/actions
# is published for third-party use, so a change here reaches further than this
# repository.
/.github/                           @OpenVTC/openvtc-admins

# The signing gate: which parent processes may drive a signature, and the
# local audit record of what was signed.
/crates/did-git-sign/src/policy.rs  @OpenVTC/openvtc-admins

# The CI authorization gate. What verify-trust accepts is what every
# downstream trust decision rests on, including the commit-object and trailer
# parsing it reads identities from.
/crates/verify-trust/               @OpenVTC/openvtc-admins

# Dependency changes reach the signing and verification paths.
/Cargo.toml                         @OpenVTC/openvtc-admins
/Cargo.lock                         @OpenVTC/openvtc-admins
